        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br> 
Space flight, due to the loss at hydrostatic pressure gradient, results in significant fluid shifts. These shifts are characterized by the movement of fluid from the lower extremities toward the thorax and upper portions of the body. The body recognizes this as a fluid overload and consequently loses some of it in the form of increased urine output and/or decreased intake. Then on re-entry, the reverse occurs. This is exacerbated by the increased gravitational forces of reentry. Another factor that could contribute to the increase in vascular pooling in the lower extremities on landing could be the loss or decrease of venous motor tone in the legs resulting in an increased compliance of the lower extremities. This can also affect the proper functioning of the cardiovascular system through pooling of blood with a decrease in cardiac output, possibly followed by orthostasis syncope.
<p>
The main objective of the experiment was to measure, quantify, and establish the time course of leg volume changes related to fluid shifts during space flight, reentry, and landing (especially upon change from horizontal to vertical posture). A leg plethysmography stocking was used to measure leg volume changes. 
<p>
APPROACH:<P>
Preflight:<BR>
Each participating crewmember was requested to make six leg volume change recordings.  Each recording consisted of a standing and supine measurement.  Training was integrated into the first preflight measurement and plethysmograph stocking fitting session. 
<p>
In flight:<BR>
Each plethysmograph measurement required two persons: one as a subject and one to mark the leg volumes.  If both crewmembers did the plethysmographs concurrently, then a total of 15 minutes was used to obtain data on both crewmembers. Otherwise, it was a two person operation with each measurement taking 15 minutes. On Mission Day 1, measurements on each subject at 3, 6, 12 and 24 hours Mission Elapsed Time (MET) were acquired. One measurement per subject near mid-mission and one near the end of the mission were also acquired.
<p>
Postflight:<BR>
Postflight data collection was performed as part of the R+0 physical exam. The crewmember was given the stocking to take home to make six additional measurements during the first week postflight, to be done at their convenience.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
Please refer to results from DSO 461.
        </div>

